- How would you apply divide-and-conquer optimization if the cost function supports it?
- How would you adapt for at-most K segments or large N with memory constraints?

## Why This Is Asked
The Trie with prefix count mechanism addresses dynamic dictionary requirements in real-time applications like chat search, testing understanding of per-node frequency tracking, deletion correctness, and memory efficiency trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with passCount and endCount for frequency tracking
- O(length) time complexity for all operations
- Memory management through node pruning
- Correct handling of duplicates and edge cases
- Amortized deletion: pruning happens during erase, no separate cleanup needed

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.pass = 0;
    this.end = 0;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    node.pass++;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) {
        node.children.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(ch);
      node.pass++;
    }
    node.end++;
  }

  exists(word) {
    const node = this._findNode(word);
    return node !== null && node.end > 0;
  }

  countPrefix(prefix) {
    const node = this._findNode(prefix);
    return node ? node.pass : 0;
  }

  erase(word) {
    if (!this.exists(word)) return false;

    let node = this.root;
    node.pass--;

    for (const ch of word) {
      const child = node.children.get(ch);
      child.pass--;
      if (child.pass === 0) {
        node.children.delete(ch);
        node = null;
        break;
      }
      node = child;
    }

    if (node) node.end--;
    return true;
  }

  _findNode(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) return null;
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    return node;
  }
}

// Usage
const trie = new Trie();
trie.insert('apple');
trie.insert('apple');
trie.insert('app');
trie.exists('apple');        // true
trie.countPrefix('app');      // 3
trie.erase('apple');
trie.exists('apple');        // true (still one left)
trie.countPrefix('app');     // 2
trie.erase('apple');
trie.exists('apple');        // false
trie.countPrefix('app');      // 1
trie.erase('nonexistent');   // false (safe)
```

## Memory Management
Nodes are allocated only when needed and automatically pruned when `pass` reaches zero during erase, ensuring no memory leaks.

## Duplicate Handling
Since `end` tracks word frequency, duplicate inserts increment the counter; duplicate erases decrement it until the count reaches zero.

## Edge Cases
- Erase on non-existent word: returns false, no changes
- Erase on word with duplicate: only decrements count, preserves other instances
- Empty string handling: root node's counters track this case

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests the integration of fundamental data structures—arrays, linked lists, hashmaps, and heaps—to solve a streaming scenario with sliding window deduplication and real-time ranking.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window deduplication per (user_id, action) using a hashmap with timestamp tracking
- Per-user score aggregation maintained in a hashmap for O(1) updates
- Global top-K ranking via max-heap with O(log K) insertion/deletion and lazy invalidation
- Efficient expiration handling using linked lists and amortized cleanup strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
class EventProcessor {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.latestByUserAction = new Map(); // (user_id,action) -> timestamp
    this.userTotals = new Map(); // user_id -> total_score
    this.userEvents = new Map(); // user_id -> linked list of (timestamp, score)
    this.maxHeap = new MaxHeap(); // (total_score, user_id)
  }

  addEvent(event) {
    const key = `${event.user_id}:${event.action}`;
    const latest = this.latestByUserAction.get(key);

    if (!latest || event.ts - latest > this.W) {
      // Update dedup map
      this.latestByUserAction.set(key, event.ts);

      // Update user total
      const currentTotal = this.userTotals.get(event.user_id) || 0;
      const newTotal = currentTotal + event.score;
      this.userTotals.set(event.user_id, newTotal);

      // Add to user's event list
      if (!this.userEvents.has(event.user_id)) {
        this.userEvents.set(event.user_id, new LinkedList());
      }
      this.userEvents.get(event.user_id).push({ts: event.ts, score: event.score});

      // Update heap
      this.maxHeap.insert({score: newTotal, user: event.user_id});
    }
  }

  getTopK(k, now) {
    this.expire(now);
    return this.maxHeap.getTopK(k);
  }

  expire(now) {
    for (const [userId, events] of this.userEvents) {
      while (events.length > 0 && now - events.head.ts > this.W) {
        const expired = events.shift();
        const currentTotal = this.userTotals.get(userId);
        const newTotal = currentTotal - expired.score;
        this.userTotals.set(userId, newTotal);

        if (newTotal > 0) {
          this.maxHeap.insert({score: newTotal, user: userId});
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
**Time:** O(1) amortized per event for updates, O(log K) for top-K retrieval
**Space:** O(N) where N is number of active users plus events within window W

## Why This Is Asked

This tests practical graph reasoning: counting shortest paths under a Dijkstra framework, handling path multiplicities without overflow, and edge-case ties.

## Key Concepts

- Dijkstra with path counting
- Tie handling on relaxation
- Complexity and edge-case handling

## Code Example

```javascript
function shortestPaths(graph, s, t) {
  const N = graph.length;
  const dist = Array(N).fill(Infinity);
  const ways = Array(N).fill(0);
  dist[s] = 0;
  ways[s] = 1;
  const pq = new MinHeap((a,b)=>a.d-b.d);
  pq.push({node:s, d:0});
  while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
     const {node:u, d} = pq.pop();
     if (d !== dist[u]) continue;
     for (const [v, w] of graph[u]) {
        const nd = dist[u] + w;
        if (nd < dist[v]) { dist[v] = nd; ways[v] = ways[u]; pq.push({node:v, d:nd}); }
        else if (nd === dist[v]) { ways[v] += ways[u]; }
     }
  }
  return { dist: dist[t], count: ways[t] };
}
```

